21
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:30,599
Two enormous wooden pots, painted black, and suspended by ass's ears, swung from

22
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:35,718
the cross trees of an old top mast, planted in front of an old doorway. The

23
00:01:35,719 --> 00:01:40,119
horns of the cross trees were sawed off on the other side, so that this old top

24
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:44,759
mass looked not a little like the gallows. Perhaps I was oversensitive to

25
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:49,158
such impressions at the time, but I could not help staring at this gallows

26
00:01:49,159 --> 00:01:53,958
with vague misgiving. A sort of cick was in my neck as I gazed up to the two

27
00:01:53,959 --> 00:01:59,878
remaining horns. Yes, two of them. One for Quiquake and one for me. It's

28
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:04,278
ominous, thinks I, a coffin, my inkeeper, upon landing in my first

29
00:02:04,279 --> 00:02:09,238
whailing port. tombstones staring at me in the whaleman's chapel and here a

30
00:02:09,239 --> 00:02:15,078
gallows and a pair of prodigious black pots too. Are these lass throwing out

31
00:02:15,079 --> 00:02:18,119
oblique hints touching toe?

32
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,878
I was called from these reflections by the sight of a freckled woman with

33
00:02:21,879 --> 00:02:26,839
yellow hair and a yellow gown, standing in the porch of the inn under a dull red

34
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:31,558
lamp swinging there that looked much like an injured eye, and carrying on a

35
00:02:31,559 --> 00:02:37,397
brisk scolding with a man in a purple woolen shirt. "Get along with ye," she

36
00:02:37,398 --> 00:02:42,519
said to the man, "or I'll be combing you." "Come on, Quiquake," said I. "All

37
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:48,039
right, there is Mrs. Hussy." And so it turned out, Mr. Hosea Hussie being from

38
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:53,238
home but leaving Mrs. Hussie entirely competent to attend to all his affairs.

39
00:02:53,239 --> 00:02:58,519
Upon making known our desires for supper and a bed, Mrs. Hussie, postponing

40
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:02,599
further scolding for the present, ushered us into a little room, and

41
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:07,477
seating us at a table spread with the relics of a recently concluded repast

42
00:03:07,478 --> 00:03:13,317
turned round to us and said, "Clam or cod?" "What's that about cods, ma'am?"

43
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:20,199
said I, with much politeness. Clam or cod? She repeated. A clam for supper. A

44
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,358
cold clam. Is that what you mean, Mrs.

45
00:03:22,359 --> 00:03:27,078
Hussy? Says I. But that's a rather cold and clammy reception in the wintertime,

46
00:03:27,079 --> 00:03:32,358
ain't it, Mrs. Hussy? But being in a great hurry to resume scolding the man

47
00:03:32,359 --> 00:03:37,078
in the purple shirt, who was waiting for it in the entry, and seeming to hear

48
00:03:37,079 --> 00:03:41,638
nothing but the word clam, Mrs. Hussy hurried towards an open door leading to

49
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:46,599
the kitchen and balling out clam for two disappeared.

50
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:52,039
"Quig," said I, "Do you think that we can make out a supper for us both on one

51
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,558
clam?"

52
00:03:53,559 --> 00:03:57,558
However, a warm and savory steam from the kitchen served to belly the

53
00:03:57,559 --> 00:04:00,199
